    "Human: You are an expert L1 IT Support Automation Engineer and OKF Playbook Architect.\n\nYour task is to analyze a raw human SOP document (which may contain GUI click instructions, manual steps, or text descriptions) and convert it into a structured, execution-ready OKF Playbook JSON schema for automated agent execution.\n\n## REGISTERED API TOOL PLATFORMS OVERVIEW\nBelow are the registered IT Tool Platforms available in the system:\n- Platform: 'solandra' — Solandra Corp IT Support platform covering CoreID Admin Console user lookup, identity verification, temporary password generation, and ticket resolution.\n- Platform: 'auth0' — Auth0 User Identity Management tools for searching users, triggering password resets, and unlocking accounts.\n- Platform: 'email' — Email service for sending ticket updates, instructions, or resolution notifications to users.\n- Platform: 'airretailer' — AirRetailer VPN and Remote Desktop (RDP) tools — VPN portal login, Cisco AnyConnect client installer/settings, VPN connection, and RDP session setup.\n- Platform: 'mock' — L1 Support Platform test API client covering user authentication, password management, MFA setup/verification, account lock/unlock, and audit logging.\n\nDYNAMIC TOOL INSPECTION RULES:\nYou have access to callable dynamic tools to inspect tool details on-demand:\n- `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)`: Fetch the exact functions, required inputs, and response schemas for a specific platform (e.g., 'solandra', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer', 'mock').\n- `list_available_platforms()`: Get the summary list of all registered platforms.\n\nSTRICT TOOL-CALLING MANDATE:\n1. Call `get_platform_tools` at most ONCE for any relevant candidate platform at the start of conversion. `get_platform_tools` returns ALL functions, input parameters, and schemas for that platform in a single payload.\n2. DO NOT call `get_platform_tools` repeatedly or per individual step.\n3. DO NOT call `list_available_platforms` if you already see the platform list above.\n4. If the raw SOP only involves native OS/system CLI commands (e.g. `chkdsk`, `ipconfig`, `sfc`, `taskkill`, `ping`) and does not match a registered platform, make ZERO (0) tool calls and proceed directly to outputting the Command Playbook JSON.\n\n## 8-STAGE DEEP CoT REASONING MANDATE (WITH 4 MANDATORY VERIFICATION GATES)\nYou MUST follow these 8 reasoning stages in exact numerical order:\n\n1. **Stage 1 (Dynamic Tool-Catalog Lookup & 4 Mandatory Verification Gates)**:\n   - If the raw SOP describes actions matching a registered platform (e.g. 'solandra', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer', 'mock'), invoke `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)` ONCE to retrieve all active tool functions and parameter schemas.\n   - Inspect the retrieved functions against the raw SOP using these 4 strict Verification Gates:\n     * **Gate 1 (Exact Function Match)**: Does the raw SOP action match an explicit function name declared in the retrieved tools?\n     * **Gate 2 (Active Platform Match)**: Is the platform name active and registered in the catalog?\n     * **Gate 3 (Problem Alignment)**: Does the candidate tool function solve the exact problem described in the raw SOP?\n     * **Gate 4 (Parameter Completeness)**: Are all 'required_inputs' declared by the tool function present or mappable to entities?\n\n    * DECISION MANDATE:\n      - IF ALL 4 Gates PASS -> Classify as a Tool Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"tool_call\"`). Populate `\"platform\"`, `\"function_name\"`, and `\"comm_inputs\"`.\n      - IF ANY Gate FAILS and the step requires a shell operation -> Classify as a Command Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"command\"`) with an exact, executable CLI shell command (with `\"shell\": \"powershell\"`). `\"command\"` MUST NEVER be null or empty.\n      - IF a step is purely manual, verbal, or phone-based (no API tool and no CLI command) -> DO NOT create an empty command step. Omit it per Mandate 11.\n      - STRICT STEP TYPE RULE: `\"step_type\"` MUST strictly be either `\"tool_call\"` or `\"command\"`. NEVER use `\"verification\"`, `\"communication\"`, or any other custom step_type string.\n\n    * PER-STEP REASONING MANDATE: For EVERY step, populate `\"step_reasoning\"` with a concise (2-4 sentence) explanation of why THIS specific output was chosen for THIS step, written for a human reviewer auditing the conversion. It MUST cover:\n      - Which of the 4 Verification Gates passed or failed, and why (i.e. why `tool_call` vs `command` was chosen here).\n      - If `tool_call`: why this exact `platform`/`function_name` was selected over any other catalog entry that looked plausible, and where each `comm_inputs` value came from (raw SOP text, an inferred entity, or a prior step's `output_variable`).\n      - If `command`: why this exact CLI command (and any `{{{{entities.*}}}}`/`{{{{variables.*}}}}` placeholders) was the correct translation of the SOP instruction.\n      - This is per-step, evidence-based reasoning distinct from the global `reasoning_chain` (which only summarizes each of the 8 stages once for the whole playbook).\n\n2. **Stage 2 (Diagnostic & Remediation Sequence Ordering)**: Ensure non-destructive search/diagnostic steps (e.g. `search_users` or `ipconfig`) come FIRST before any corrective or account modification commands.\n\n3. **Stage 3 (Precondition & Variable Extraction)**: Identify dynamic placeholders and ENFORCE strict double-brace format:\n   - For `command` steps: Insert placeholders in command text (e.g. `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.executable}}}}`).\n   - For `tool_call` steps: You MUST populate all required input parameters mentioned in the SOP (e.g. `current_password`, `email`, `user_id`) inside `comm_inputs` (e.g. `{{{{ \"password\": \"{{{{entities.current_password}}}}\" }}}}`).\n   - Use `{{{{entities.var_name}}}}` for pre-configured entities (`{{{{entities.user_id}}}}`, `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.email}}}}`, `{{{{entities.current_password}}}}`) and `{{{{variables.var_name}}}}` for runtime step outputs. DO NOT use un-nested single braces.\n\n4. **Stage 4 (Strict SOP Command Fidelity & Zero Unwritten Action Hallucinations)**: Extract ONLY actions and tools explicitly written in the raw SOP text. DO NOT invent, fabricate, or add unwritten steps (such as `send_email`, `search_users`, or notification steps) unless explicitly requested in the raw SOP text. If not explicitly specified in the raw SOP, set `\"fallback_command\": null`.\n\n5. **Stage 5 (Verification & Output Parsing Instructions)**: Verification and diagnostic check steps described in the raw SOP MUST NOT be created as separate step types. Instead, create a `tool_call` or `command` step and write clear regex/string inspection rules for `output_parse_instruction` and `expected_output_contains`:\n   - For `command` steps: Inspect CLI stdout (e.g. \"If output contains 'SUCCESS', set 'kill_confirmed' to 'true'\").\n   - For `tool_call` steps: Use the exact JSON field names declared in the tool's `Response Schema` (e.g. \"If response contains status='unlocked', set 'unlock_success' to 'true' and store response.user_id in variables.user_id\").\n\n6. **Stage 6 (100% Automated Action Recall Mandate)**: You MUST extract EVERY automated diagnostic, query, and remediation step described in the SOP. Non-automated verbal/phone actions that have no tool or CLI command must not be added as empty command steps.\n\n7. **Stage 7 (Conditional Branch Mapping & execute_if)**: If the raw SOP specifies conditional skip instructions, map this logic into valid `execute_if` boolean expressions (e.g., `execute_if: variables.account_found == 'true'`).\n\n8. **Stage 8 (Dynamic Timeout Allocation by Action Scope)**: Assign `timeout_seconds` dynamically: Quick Info Read / API Call (30s), System Scan / Diagnostic Audit (90s), Full Repair / Heavy Job (300s).\n\n9. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit GUI Window Setup Steps)**: DO NOT generate non-executable GUI setup steps like \"Open Command Prompt\" or \"Open Web Browser\". Start directly with functional actions.\n\n10. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Zero Unwritten Action Addition)**: You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from inventing or adding steps/tool calls (such as `send_email` or `search_users`) that are NOT explicitly stated in the raw SOP document.\n\n11. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit Manual Human/Verbal Confirmation Steps)**: Human/verbal/ticket confirmation checks, manual admin approvals, verbal phone relay, and user clarification steps are non-automated. DO NOT generate empty command steps (`\"command\": null`) for them. Omit them completely so that EVERY step in the playbook is 100% executable (either a valid API `tool_call` or a valid non-null CLI `command`).\n\n\n## OUTPUT JSON SCHEMA MANDATE\nCRITICAL FORMATTING MANDATE:\n- All keys and string values MUST be enclosed in standard DOUBLE QUOTES (\").\n- NEVER use Python single quotes (') for dictionary keys or strings.\n- Return raw, valid JSON only matching this schema:\n```json\n{{\n    \"reasoning_chain\": [\n        \"[Stage 1 Verification Gates]: Gate 1 (Function match) -> Gate 2 (Platform active) -> Gate 3 (Problem aligned) -> Gate 4 (Params complete) -> Result: tool_call | command\",\n        \"[Stage 2 Sequencing]: Ordered search/diagnostic steps before remediation\",\n        \"[Stage 3 Variables]: Formatted {{{{entities.var}}}} placeholders\",\n        \"[Stage 4 Fallbacks]: Mapped fallback actions\",\n        \"[Stage 5 Verification]: Added output parse instructions\",\n        \"[Stage 6 Recall]: Preserved 100% of diagnostic steps\",\n        \"[Stage 7 Branching]: Mapped execute_if conditions\",\n        \"[Stage 8 Timeouts]: Assigned timeout_seconds\"\n    ],\n    \"sop_id\": \"SOP-{{file_name_slug}}-001\",\n    \"title\": \"Title of Playbook\",\n    \"platform\": \"primary platform (e.g. solandra, auth0, airretailer, mock, system)\",\n    \"category\": \"network | storage | system | logins | software | hardware\",\n    \"target_os\": \"windows | linux\",\n    \"description\": \"Comprehensive overview of what this playbook does\",\n    \"required_entities\": [\"user_id\", \"email\", \"hostname\"],\n    \"trigger_words\": [\"unlock\", \"account\", \"login\", \"platform_name\"],\n    \"steps\": [\n        {{\n            \"step_number\": 1,\n            \"sop_step_description\": \"Detailed step objective\",\n            \"step_type\": \"tool_call | command\",\n            \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 result and why tool_call/command was chosen; why this exact platform/function/command and comm_inputs/placeholders were used over alternatives.\",\n            \"platform\": \"platform name from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"function_name\": \"exact_function_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"comm_inputs\": {{\n                \"user_id\": \"{{{{entities.user_id}}}}\"\n            }},\n            \"command\": \"ipconfig /all (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"shell\": \"powershell (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"fallback_command\": null,\n            \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n            \"expected_output_contains\": \"unlocked\",\n            \"timeout_seconds\": 30,\n            \"output_variable\": \"step_result\",\n            \"output_parse_instruction\": \"Inspect output/JSON response and set status flags.\",\n            \"execute_if\": null,\n            \"is_dangerous\": false,\n            \"danger_warning\": null\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n```\n\n## RAW SOP DOCUMENT TEXT TO TRANSLATE:\nReset a Locked-Out User's Password via CoreID Admin Console\nL1 Escalation Runbook — Solandra Corp IT Support\nsop_id\nSOL-PWD-RESET-L1-002\nresolution_type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a ticketing system, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The top-left corner has the text \"CoreID\" in white, followed by \"Admin Console\" in smaller text below it.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   The main section of the image displays a ticket with the following details:\n        *   **Ticket #**: SOL-84213\n        *   **Category**: Password Reset - Escalated\n        *   **Requester**: J. Employee\n        *   **Employee ID**: SOL-04821\n        *   **Manager**: A. Menon\n        *   **Department**: Finance\n        *   **Opened**: Today, 10:42 AM\n*   **Menu Options**\n    *   The dark blue sidebar on the left has several menu options:\n        *   **Dashboard**\n        *   **Tickets**\n        *   **Users**\n        *   **Reports**\n        *   **Settings**\n*   **Ticket Status**\n    *   The ticket is currently open, as indicated by the \"Opened\" timestamp.\n*   **User Information**\n    *   The user who opened the ticket is J. Employee, with an employee ID of SOL-04821.\n    *   The manager assigned to the ticket is A. Menon.\n    *   The department responsible for the ticket is Finance.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a ticketing system with a ticket open for password reset escalation, assigned to J. Employee in the Finance department, with A. Menon as the manager. The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's profile information displayed. The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The CoreID Admin Console is displayed on the left side of the image.\n\t+ It has a dark blue background with white text.\n\t+ The menu options are: Admin Console, Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, Settings.\n* **User Profile**\n\t+ The user's profile information is displayed in the center of the image.\n\t+ The user's name is SOL-04821.\n\t+ Their role is J. Employee.\n\t+ The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n\t+ The user's department is Finance.\n* **Locked Button**\n\t+ A locked button is displayed next to the user's profile information.\n\t+ The button is red and has a white lock icon.\n\t+ The button is labeled \"Locked\".\n\nThe image suggests that the user's account is currently locked, and the support agent will need to investigate why this is the case. The user's profile information is also displayed, which may be useful for further investigation.\n\n**Extracted Text**\n\n* None\n\n**Errors and Warnings**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n\n* Category: Account/authentication\n* Severity: Medium\n* Reason: The user's account is locked, which may indicate a security issue or a problem with the user's authentication credentials.\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**\n\n* **Observed Facts**\n\t+ The user's name is SOL-04821.\n\t+ Their role is J. Employee.\n\t+ The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n\t+ The user's department is Finance.\n\t+ The user's account is locked.\n* **Inference**\n\t+ The user's account may be locked due to a security issue or a problem with their authentication credentials.\n\t+ The support agent may need to investigate further to determine the cause of the lockout.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, and the text is legible. The user's profile information is also displayed, which provides useful context for the issue.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white box in the center titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\". The box contains a list of options, including \"Employee ID matches ticket\", \"Manager name matches ticket\", and \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n\n*   The CoreID Admin Console is visible on the left side of the image.\n*   The white box in the center of the image is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n*   The box contains a list of options, including \"Employee ID matches ticket\", \"Manager name matches ticket\", and \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n*   The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" option is checked.\n*   The \"Manager name matches ticket\" option is also checked.\n*   The \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\" option is not checked.\n\n**EXTRACTED TEXT:**\n\n*   None\n\n**ERRORS AND WARNINGS:**\n\n*   None\n\n**LIKELY ISSUE CATEGORY AND SEVERITY:**\n\n*   Category: Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**OBSERVED FACTS VS INFERENCE:**\n\n*   OBSERVED FACTS:\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is visible on the left side of the image.\n    *   The white box in the center of the image is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n    *   The box contains a list of options, including \"Employee ID matches ticket\", \"Manager name matches ticket\", and \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n    *   The \"Employee ID matches ticket\" option is checked.\n    *   The \"Manager name matches ticket\" option is also checked.\n    *   The \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\" option is not checked.\n*   INFERENCE:\n    *   The user is attempting to verify an employee's identity.\n    *   The employee's ID and manager's name match the ticket.\n    *   The last 4 digits of the phone number on file do not match.\n\n**CONFIDENCE SCORE & NOTES:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the text is not legible.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a login page for an employee to reset their password. The page is titled \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" and has a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\". \n\n*   The page is titled \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n*   There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n*   The page has a white background with black text.\n*   The page has a gray bar at the top with the words \"CoreID Admin Console\" in white text.\n*   The page has a gray bar on the left side with the words \"Users\" in white text.\n*   The page has a gray bar at the bottom with the words \"Settings\" in white text.\n\nThe page appears to be a standard login page for an employee to reset their password. The blue button suggests that the user can generate a temporary password, which is a common feature on many login pages. The gray bars on the left and bottom of the page provide navigation options for the user. Overall, the page is clean and easy to use, with clear instructions for the user to follow. \n\nThere are no error messages or warnings visible on the page. The page appears to be functioning correctly, with no obvious issues or problems. The user can simply click on the blue button to generate a temporary password and proceed with the login process. \n\nThe likely issue category is \"Software/application\", as the page is related to resetting a password for an employee. The severity of the issue is likely \"Low\", as the page is functioning correctly and there are no obvious errors or warnings. The confidence score is 0.95, indicating a high level of confidence in the interpretation of the image. The confidence level is \"High\", as the image is clear and easy to understand. The confidence reason is that the image is well-lit and the text is legible, making it easy to interpret. \n\nOBSERVED FACTS:\n\n*   The page is titled \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n*   There is a blue button that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n*   The page has a white background with black text.\n*   The page has a gray bar at the top with the words \"CoreID Admin Console\" in white text.\n*   The page has a gray bar on the left side with the words \"Users\" in white text.\n*   The page has a gray bar at the bottom with the words \"Settings\" in white text.\n\nINFERENCE:\n\n*   The page is a standard login page for an employee to reset their password.\n*   The blue button suggests that the user can generate a temporary password.\n*   The gray bars on the left and bottom of the page provide navigation options for the user.\n*   The page appears to be clean and easy to use, with clear instructions for the user to follow.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   Dashboard\n    *   Tickets\n    *   Users\n    *   Reports\n    *   Settings\n\nThe image suggests that the ticket has been resolved, and the resolution notes indicate that the identity has been verified, a temporary password has been issued, and the force-change-at-next-sign-in feature has been enabled. There are no error messages or warnings visible in the image.\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Category: Software/Application\n*   Severity: Medium\n\nThe issue appears to be related to the CoreID Admin Console, and the severity is medium because the resolution notes indicate that the identity has been verified, but there are still some checks to be completed.\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n    *   The status is resolved.\n    *   The resolution notes indicate that the identity has been verified and a temporary password has been issued.\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The issue may be related to authentication or authorization.\n    *   The temporary password may be required for the user to access the system.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   Confidence Score: 0.95\n*   Confidence Level: High\n*   Confidence Reason: The image is clear, and the text is legible. The resolution notes provide specific details about the issue and its resolution.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog](/kev)[Report A Cyber Issue](/report)\n\n[![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Security and Resilience](/sites/default/files/images/SVG/header_logo_tagline_update.svg)![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Secur\n\n## PREVIOUS ATTEMPT ERRORS (MUST FIX ON THIS RETRY):\n- Scenario 3/4 Validation Error (Attempt 1): [\"Layer 1 Input Error: Step 2 function 'verify_caller_identity' missing required input parameter 'phone_last4' in comm_inputs.\"]"
